{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.7,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["J12", "U5"],
      "pins": ["J12.A4", "U5.1", "U5.2"],
      "nets": ["VBUS", "+5V"],
      "observed": "On the MCU sheet the USB-C receptacle VBUS pin (J12, pin A4) carries the net label 'VBUS' on the same wire that is tied to a '+5V' power symbol. On the Power sheet 'VBUS' is the mux OUTPUT net (U5 pin 1) and '+5V' is the mux IN2 net (U5 pin 2). Because both names appear on the USB VBUS wire, the +5V input net and the VBUS mux-output net are merged into one net, so U5.OUT is shorted to U5.IN2 and to the USB 5V pin.",
      "expected": "Spec: 'J12 ... VBUS pins (A4/A9/B4/B9) → +5V net → U5 pin 2/2_1 (IN2)' and 'OUT ... onto the VBUS net (the board's internal, unregulated/pre-buck rail — note this net is named VBUS in the netlist but is distinct from the USB +5V net upstream of the mux).' The USB VBUS pin must be on the +5V net only; 'VBUS' must be reserved for the mux output.",
      "impact": "The TPS2121 power mux cannot arbitrate: its OUT is tied to its own IN2 and to the USB-C VBUS pin. When the 12 V barrel jack is connected the mux output back-feeds 12 V into the USB-C port and the +5V input path is defeated; the autoswitching supply architecture is broken.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "MCU sheet (page 3): J12 pin A4 wire shows '+5V' power symbol and 'VBUS' net label; Power sheet (page 7): U5 pin1 OUT='VBUS', pin2 IN2='+5V'"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "J12 ... VBUS pins (A4/A9/B4/B9) → +5V net → U5 pin 2/2_1 (IN2) ... note this net is named VBUS in the netlist but is distinct from the USB +5V net upstream of the mux"}
      ],
      "confidence": 0.72
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["U1", "U2", "U3", "U4"],
      "pins": ["U1.18", "U1.19", "U1.20", "U1.21", "U1.22"],
      "nets": ["CS0", "SCK", "CS1", "CS2"],
      "observed": "On the MCU sheet the SPI/CS net labels are attached one GPIO position too low: CS0→U1 pin18 (GPIO3), SCK→pin19 (GPIO4), CS1→pin20 (GPIO5), CS2→pin21 (GPIO6), while pin22 (GPIO7) is left unconnected (SO is correctly on GPIO10).",
      "expected": "Spec/firmware: 'SCK ... tied to ... the ESP32 (U1 GPIO5/SCK, GPIO10/SO). Each chip has its own independent chip-select ... CS0/CS1/CS2, driven by ESP32 GPIO4/GPIO6/GPIO7 respectively' (i.e. SCK=GPIO5, CS0=GPIO4, CS1=GPIO6, CS2=GPIO7; GPIO3 is not used in the intended design).",
      "impact": "The SPI clock and the three chip-selects are routed to the wrong ESP32 GPIOs relative to the firmware: firmware drives SCK on GPIO5 but the board routes GPIO5 to CS1, drives CS0 on GPIO4 but the board routes GPIO4 to SCK, and firmware CS2 (GPIO7) is dead while GPIO3 (unused by firmware) carries CS0. Thermocouple reads would fail or select the wrong channel.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "MCU sheet (page 3): U1 right side — pin18 GPIO3/ADC1_CH3→CS0, pin19 GPIO4/ADC1_CH4→SCK, pin20 GPIO5/ADC2_CH0→CS1, pin21 GPIO6→CS2, pin22 GPIO7 unconnected"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "SCK ... tied to ... the ESP32 (U1 GPIO5/SCK, GPIO10/SO). Each chip has its own independent chip-select: ~{CS} ... goes to separate nets CS0/CS1/CS2, driven by ESP32 GPIO4/GPIO6/GPIO7 respectively"}
      ],
      "confidence": 0.6
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The drawing shows two deviations from the intended design: the USB-C VBUS pin is labeled 'VBUS' (the mux-output rail) instead of '+5V', merging the +5V input net with the mux output and shorting U5.OUT to U5.IN2; and the SPI SCK/CS0/CS1/CS2 net labels are shifted one GPIO position relative to the firmware (SCK on GPIO4, CS0 on GPIO3, CS2's GPIO7 left open), which would break thermocouple communication."
}

